{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And he said to them: You are spies; to see the nakedness of the land you have come.</b> The commentators struggled with this accusation and with the variation of expression, in that he mentioned \"spies\" and \"the nakedness of the land\" both together, and sometimes \"spies\" alone, and sometimes he would mention \"the nakedness of the land\" alone. Are they not the same thing? And in the Akeidah and the Mahar[i?]a you will find one approach to all the difficulties according to their custom, for the approach of both of them follows the majority. And what seems close to me to say on this matter is that there were here accusations concerning two things, in the manner explained in the book Toldot Yitzchak, that there are two kinds of spies: one is one who spies on the men of war — what they are doing and what they are saying and where they will go; the second is one who spies on a particular place and from which direction the city is vulnerable to conquest. And corresponding to the first he said \"You are spies,\" and corresponding to the second he said \"To see the nakedness of the land you have come.\"" "And according to this approach, it appears possible to settle all the variations in these verses, because these two — each one is more difficult than its fellow, for Joseph needed to give a reason and a rationale for each accusation. For on the one hand, since he saw them concealing themselves among the arrivals, he said \"You are spies\" — for this is a decisive proof that you are hiding among the people in order to hear what the people relate one to another; therefore you scatter among many people, so that this one hears here and that one in another place, and this is a great proof against which there is no mouth to reply. And on the other hand, since he saw them entering through ten gates of the city, he suspected them of \"to see the nakedness of the land you have come.\" But this is nevertheless not as great a proof as the first, for you could answer and say that you have no connection one with another, and each one came from a different road, and it happened by chance that within the city you gathered together — for it is not far-fetched that ten agents would gather together to buy grain. But the matter of spying does not depend on this, for even if you say that is so, nevertheless the fact that each one of you conceals himself like a thief — this is proof that each one of you is a spy. Therefore I say that both of them are found in you, and the one is proof for the other. And not only are you spies, but even to see the nakedness of the land you are suspected. Even though on this you have an excuse, nevertheless, since you are certainly spies, then to see the nakedness of the land is also close to certain."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {0 "Mahar[i?]a: the Hebrew reads וּמַהֲרִי״א, likely an abbreviation for a rabbinic authority; left unresolved as the witness is ambiguous."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:13.596036889Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}